{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Fixed on branch `drift-fd2b-eebc`, commit `d043be48dcba`, pushed for review with `jaded-pugnacious-isopod` (they share `.github/workflows/terraform-drift.yml`, so they are one change).\n\n## Scope decision: SPLIT INTO JOBS, not reorder\n\nThe ticket offered two defensible options (reorder plan-before-fmt, or `continue-on-error` + a later gate). I took a third that dominates both: `terraform fmt -check` moved into its own top-level job `fmt`, with **no `needs:` relationship** to the `plan` job.\n\nReasoning, against the ACs:\n\n- **AC1** — reordering satisfies it only for the *first* failure. Within one job, a fmt failure still fails the job that carries the drift verdict, and a later maintainer re-ordering steps re-creates the bug. Separate jobs make the coupling structurally absent rather than conventionally avoided.\n- **AC2** — `continue-on-error` would have made the fmt failure non-fatal, which is the same \"reported as success\" shape this ticket is about. Its own job fails outright instead. A test asserts no fmt step carries `continue-on-error`.\n- **AC3** — this is what actually decided it. Reordering leaves ONE job name carrying both outcomes, so \"whitespace is wrong\" and \"we did not look for drift\" remain indistinguishable in the checks list without opening logs. Two jobs make a red run self-describing: `fmt` red = whitespace, `plan` red = drift (or an unprovable tree).\n- **AC4** — `tests/unit/test_terraform_drift_workflow_shape.py` parses the workflow and pins: no fmt step shares the plan job; the plan job does not `needs:` any fmt job; fmt survives with `-check` and without `continue-on-error`; fmt and plan are distinct job names. It was RED on the pre-fix workflow for exactly those reasons before the fix existed.\n\n## ADR 0114\n\nRead it; it does not gate this change. It governs adding or promoting a check into the required `Verified` vote aggregate in `.github/workflows/gerrit-verify.yaml`. Neither `fmt` nor `plan` here is in that aggregate (grep of `gerrit-verify.yaml` for `terraform` returns nothing), so this workflow cannot lock its own repair or revert out of the landing path — the self-lock ADR 0114 exists to prevent is unreachable here. Its *spirit* did shape the design though: the formatting signal is deliberately demoted out of the drift verdict's path rather than left blocking, which is the same restraint one step earlier.\n\n## Not done\n\n`terraform validate` still precedes `plan` in the same job, deliberately. Unlike fmt it is a genuine precondition: an invalid configuration cannot be planned at all, so failing there is not a missed plan, it is an impossible one. Noted in the workflow comment so a future reader does not \"fix\" it by symmetry.\n\nNothing was applied. `terraform apply` remains operator-only."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788574418372527951,"uuid":"8537f21a-4db1-47d2-806e-0574a3bd3178"}